extra savings using the code 9TO5TOYS6OFF at checkout.\n\n### Bluetti\u2019s New Year Sale Elite 400 offers:\n\n  * Elite 400 (3,840Wh) power station: $1,299 (Reg. $2,999)\n  * Elite 400 with alternator charger 1: $1,599 (Reg. $3,299)\n  * Elite 400 with 350W solar panel: $1,799 (Reg. $3,698)\n  * Elite 400 with 2x 200W solar panels: $1,849 (Reg. $3,798)\n  * Elite 400 with 2x 350W solar panels: $2,048 (Reg. $4,598)\n\n### Bluetti\u2019s New Year Sale offers for compact mobile power:\n\n### Bluetti\u2019s New Year Sale offers for mobile power hubs:\n\n### Bluetti\u2019s New Year Sale home backup offers:\n\nYou can browse the entire Bluetti New Year Sale on the landing page here \u2013 but remember to use those exclusive codes for the best deals! Bluetti has created plenty of buzz at CES 2026, where the brand launched its new Alternator & Solar Dual DC Charger 2 + bundles \u2013 and of course, we secured our readers some exclusive bonus savings \u2013 all starting from $332. You can also check out our coverage of Bluetti\u2019s winter-ready power station deals starting from $759.\n\nImage: man riding Tenways e-bike down street\n\n## Tenways\u2019 37-pound CGO600 Pro e-bikes drop to $1,599 with $118 FREE bundles + 50% off range extender, more\n\nTenways has switched up its New Year e-bike sale offers, with the event currently providing you with up to $600 discounts on models, as well as an extra $200 savings when buying two together. Aside from the more premium models seeing continuing deals from last week, there\u2019s also others like Tenways\u2019 CGO600 Pro Belt Drive Commuter e-bike at $1,599 shipped, while the CGO600 Pro 8-speed chain-drive e-bike is also down at $1,599 shipped. Both of these variants also come with $118 FREE bundle package alongside the option to score a Power Bank (range extender) for an additional $155 (normally $309). These e-bikes normally fetch $1,899 outside of sales, which we\u2019ve mostly seen dropped to $1,599 in our post-tariff market, while select events, like Black Friday, saw the price dip $100 lower. If you want to learn more about this particular e-bike, or browse the full lineup of deals, be sure to check out our original coverage of this switched-up New Year sale here.\n```\n\n**5\\. Reference ID\\:** `turn908543news6`\n\n**Title\\:** A Portable Power Station Will Save Your Summer Camping Trip. Here\u2019s Which to Buy\n\n**URL\\:** \ue200url\ue202https\\://www\\.wired.com/gallery/best-portable-power-stations/\ue202https://www.wired.com/gallery/best-portable-power-stations/\ue201\n\n**Excerpt (full)\\:**\n\n```text\nSpecs\n---\nCapacity: 288 watt-hours\nMax Output: 300 watts\nAC Charging time: 2 hours\nPorts: 2 x USB-C, 1 x USB-A, 1 x Car socket, 1 x AC\n\n  * ## Best Power Station for Drone Pilots\n\n    * Image: Front of a portable power station\n\n    * Image: Close up of a hand on the handle of a portable power station\n\n    * Image: Close up of the outlets on a portable power station\n\n    It makes sense that the maker of some of the best drones would create a power station that helps keep them powered up. After all, flight time is often very limited due to battery sizes, and having a power station right there lets you get back in the air faster. The DJI Power 2000 has a typical design with molded handles, though it weighs around 49 pounds. A trio of features makes the DJI Power 2000 stand out. Power Stations: Frequently Asked Questions\n\nAccordionItemContainerButton\n\nHow Expensive Are Portable Power Stations?\n\nPortable power stations can be very expensive, but discounts, sales, and deals are common. If you can afford to wait, you can likely get your chosen power station for less than the listed MSRP.\n\nWhat Capacity Do I Need?\n\nFigure out how much power you need. The capacity is listed in watt-hours (Wh) or sometimes kilowatt-hours (kWh). Manufacturers will often display how many hours a TV or other appliance can run, but you need to calculate how much power your own devices actually use.\n\nHow Portable Are Power Stations?\n\nThe term \u201cportable\u201d is sometimes stretched. Batteries are heavy. Larger-capacity power stations are typically on wheels and have telescopic handles, and they are still tough to cart around. If you want something you can carry on foot, you may need to compromise on capacity.\n\nWhat Can You Run on a Portable Power Station?\n\nPower stations can charge small gadgets like phones and laptops and run lighting. Many can handle small appliances like mini-fridges or TVs. For power tools, AC units, or other high-wattage appliances, check the maximum output and surge capacity.\n\nHow Do I Charge a Portable Power Station?\n\nPower stations can be charged from a wall outlet. Some can also charge via a car port or solar panels. Check that the required ports and cables are included.\n\nHow Long Does a Portable Power Station Take to Charge?\n\nLarge-capacity power stations can take a long time to recharge. Check charging speeds from wall power and other inputs. Some support fast charging from multiple inputs.\n\nWhat Ports Should I Look for in a Portable Power Station?\n\nCheck the maximum charging rate and supported charging standards for each port. These may include USB-C, USB-A, car ports, barrel connectors, and solar inputs.\n\nHow Many Years Do Portable Power Stations Last?\n\nPower stations typically last between three and 10 years, depending on use and maintenance. Avoid leaving them fully discharged for extended periods. Warranties typically range from two to five years.\n\nWhat Battery Types Are Commonly Used in Portable Power Stations?\n\nCommon technologies include lithium-ion (Li-ion), including lithium nickel manganese cobalt oxide (Li-NMC) and lithium iron phosphate (LiFePO4 or LFP). LFP batteries generally last longer and are less prone to combustion, while NMC batteries have higher energy density but degrade faster. Can You Use a Portable Power Station as a UPS?\n\nSome power stations can act as an uninterruptible power supply (UPS); others are emergency power supplies (EPS). UPS systems switch to battery power almost instantly during an outage, while EPS systems may take longer.\n\nWhat Is the Difference Between a Power Bank and a Portable Power Station?\n\nPower banks are typically compact and designed for phones and small gadgets. Power stations have much larger capacities and can potentially run appliances and larger devices.\n\nHow to Care for Your Power Station\n\nAvoid fully draining the battery when possible. Store it in a cool, dry place and use the supplied charging cables. Check environmental limits before exposing it to dust, rain, or extreme temperatures.\n\nA permanently installed home battery may be a better solution if portability isn't needed. It can be professionally wired into your electrical panel and automate energy storage and usage. Consider reading a guide on how to buy a home battery.\n```\n\n**6\\. Reference ID\\:** `turn908543news7`\n\n**Title\\:** Bluetti exclusive Early Prime Day Elite 200 V2 power station \\$759, Navee G5 Max e-scooter \\$650 low, EGO electric mini bike, more\n\n**URL\\:** \ue200url\ue202https\\://electrek.co/2026/06/11/bluetti-prime-day-elite-200-v2-power-station-navee-g5-max-e-scooter-ego-electric-mini-bike-more/\ue202https://electrek.co/2026/06/11/bluetti-prime-day-elite-200-v2-power-station-navee-g5-max-e-scooter-ego-electric-mini-bike-more/\ue201\n\n**Excerpt\\:** NO EXCERPT\n\n**7\\. Reference ID\\:** `turn908543news8`\n\n**Title\\:** July 4th Green Deals: Bluetti Summer Power Sale exclusives, Hiboy EX11 full suspension e-bike \\$700 off, EcoFlow flash sale, and much more\n\n**URL\\:** \ue200url\ue202https\\://electrek.co/2026/07/03/july-4th-bluetti-summer-power-sale-hiboy-ex11-full-suspension-e-bike-ecoflow-more/\ue202https://electrek.co/2026/07/03/july-4th-bluetti-summer-power-sale-hiboy-ex11-full-suspension-e-bike-ecoflow-more/\ue201\n\n**Excerpt\\:** NO EXCERPT\n\n**8\\. Reference ID\\:** `turn908543sear